Register Code-Generation Instruction Contracts:

  Source Proposal:
    docs/RegisterCodegenPolicyProposal.md

  Goal:
    Replace paired wrapper/raw comparisons with Catch2-defined instruction contracts for production-code fixtures, scheduled by CTest and matched by LLVM FileCheck.
    Define each method's primary case once across its supported compiler matrix; add only supplemental compiler-specific facts.
    Preserve coverage, independently owned correctness evidence, and useful build provenance without retaining instruction hashes as acceptance policy.

  Execution Contract:
    ☐ Treat the source proposal as authoritative; implement this plan without expanding the production API or algorithm scope.
    ☐ Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not commit, clean, or overwrite unrelated work as part of execution.
    ☐ Run build and test tools through subagents, as required by the repository instructions supplied by the user.
    ☐ Add the required documentation comments to methods/classes and logical explanations to nontrivial methods; keep composition roots thin and group related methods as instructed.
    ☐ Keep phase terminology in planning documents only.
    ☐ Record commands, compiler/tool identities, selected configurations, results, and artifact paths for each completed increment; distinguish source review, harness tests, compiled codegen checks, behavioral tests, and consumer/package evidence.
    ☐ Perform a second review against the proposal before marking an increment complete; leave unchecked any task whose required evidence is missing.

  Scope and Non-Goals:
    - Cover Register, RegisterMask, PartialRegister, PartialRegisterMask, and the existing API partial-transfer and method-attribute checks that depend on retired machinery.
    - Preserve runtime, constexpr, availability, compile-failure, layout, and external-consumer test responsibilities; independent behavioral reference implementations are not codegen duplicates to delete.
    - Use CMake for compilation/dependencies and Catch2 discovery, Catch2 for composite assertions and diagnostics, CTest for scheduling/reporting, and FileCheck for expectations; introduce no Python or lit requirement.
    - Do not add a general assembly-equivalence engine, full-listing golden baselines, or a separate retained-hash exception mechanism.
    - A pass establishes the declared instruction properties for the exercised case and configuration, not semantic correctness, optimal code, measured speed, or equality with another implementation.
    - Existing gates remain authoritative until validated replacements are selected at cutover. Temporary coexistence is migration scaffolding, not the final design.

  Assumptions and Open Decisions:
    - GCC/Clang -O1 is a candidate, not a prequalified setting. Phase 4 selects and validates exact low-optimization options for every supported driver, including MSVC optimization/inlining settings.
    - Wholly unoptimized coverage is intentionally unresolved. Phase 4 defines meaningful checked contracts and diagnostic-only observations without claiming a worst-case performance bound.
    - This plan follows the proposal's recommended production-optimization companion checks; their exact flags and applicability are recorded in Phase 4.
    - Phases 1-3 retain their recorded inventory, extraction, and original CMake-runner evidence. The approved Catch2 direction supersedes the outer runner design; Phase 3A must validate its replacement before Phase 4 begins.
    - Proposed component paths follow the proposal. The migration inventory's storage format is an implementation implication to settle in Phase 1, not an additional public API.
    - If the new checks reveal a production defect or an unsupported performance requirement, record it and resolve the stated support boundary separately; do not silently alter production algorithms or weaken the checks to complete this plan.

  Phase 1 - Inventory Coverage and Specify Case Ownership:
    Depends on: the source proposal and current qualification documents.
    Outcome: an explicit migration inventory and case model that can drive registration without rediscovering legacy coverage.

    Deliverables:
      - codegen/MigrationInventory.md: closed case sets, identities, geometry/matrices, contract families, supplemental facts, and independent validation ownership.
      - codegen/IntegrationInventory.md: legacy consumers, artifact/provisioning ownership, downstream resolution tasks, and task-to-document traceability.
    Evidence scope:
      - Source baseline: 065f15f4d5b7734d128718030fe54791619ea8ee; PowerShell 7.6.6.
      - Source/document inventory only; no compiled codegen, behavioral, package, or tool-provisioning qualification is claimed.
      - Second review reconciled the proposal and qualification boundaries with the fixture/CMake declarations, including the distinct 0x1B Register and 0x35 PartialRegister multi-SAD immediates.

    ☑ Read RegisterQualification.md, PartialRegisterQualification.md, and RegisterImplementationMatrix.md to inventory the current support and evidence boundaries; distinguish existing claims from the proposed replacement claims.
    ☑ Inventory existing fixture symbols, comparison records, instruction checks, and validation owners in tests/codegen and cmake/development/{RegisterCodegen,PartialRegisterCodegen,MethodFlagsCodegen}.cmake.
      ☑ Include isolated operations/type matrices, composition, transfers, special members, reassignment, immediate and complete shifts, specialized operations, FMA, rearrangements/conversions, pressure, opaque calls, and ABI/consumer boundaries.
      ☑ Include API partial transfers, method attributes, and observational platform-default ABI records; identify every consumer of the old comparator, profile data, indexes, and validators before proposing removal.
      ☑ Record supported compiler families/drivers and versions, ISA/width combinations, element types, active extents, immediate parameters, FMA modes, and ABI/configuration dimensions.
    ☑ Choose and create a durable migration inventory mapping each existing case to its replacement primary case, emitted function or ABI fixture group, shared contract, supplemental facts, applicability, and validation owner.
      ☑ Record a primary case once with its execution matrix; do not create separately authored full definitions for MSVC, Clang, and GCC.
      ☑ Preserve existing availability distinctions and identify expected emitted functions independently of disassembly output.
      ☑ Identify established stronger compiler-specific properties that must remain checked even when they are not universal.
    ☑ Specify stable logical case IDs, explicit emitted-symbol identities, configuration IDs, and the relationship between reusable contract families and method expectations.
      ☑ Define supplemental checks as additive requirements only; a selected compiler must still execute the primary case.
      ☑ Permit one shared supplemental declaration for compilers with the same additional fact.
      ☑ Distinguish constant/identity cases with valid minimal bodies from missing functions; identify ABI cases that require caller/callee fixture groups.
    ☑ Define the initial contract set: register-only, load/operate/store, immediate operations, FMA behavior, ABI boundaries, and targeted regressions, selecting only applicable restrictions per case.
    ☑ Identify existing build/test entry points, artifact/provenance ownership, CI upload rules, and provisioning locations that must integrate with the replacement.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☑ Every existing in-scope case has a mapped replacement and evidence owner, including observations and adjacent consumers of retired machinery.
      ☑ The case model contains no compiler-specific copies of complete primary definitions and no unrecorded loss of stronger expectations.
      ☑ Open implementation choices have named resolution tasks below; the inventory does not present unknown settings as qualified.

    Completion audit:
      - Independent audit-stage-completion review: fully satisfied for the source-inventory scope; no confirmed defects or required evidence gaps.
      - Subagent validation: git diff --check passed; all 11 local Markdown links resolved; fixture expansion and legacy-consumer ownership checks passed.
      - Confirmed 442 isolated operation/type cells per width and 56 partial ABI identity boundaries plus eight named shapes per width.
      - Tool selection, real-object instruction bounds, optimization settings, and cutover validation remain explicitly owned by later phases.

  Phase 2 - Provision Tools and Validate Complete Function Extraction:
    Depends on: Phase 1 case and artifact identities.
    Outcome: a narrowly scoped object-inspection component whose successful extraction is supported by positive and negative evidence.

    ☑ Select FileCheck and the disassembler explicitly, record their versions/identities, and add development-only discovery/provisioning in the relevant local and CI/container environments.
      ☑ Fail clearly when required tools are absent or unsupported; do not silently select an unrelated PATH executable or skip required qualification.
      ☑ Keep these tools out of installed library/runtime dependencies and introduce no Python or lit requirement.
    ☑ Implement cmake/codegen/ExtractFunction.cmake with the proposal's single responsibility and an explicit input/output contract.
      ☑ Resolve expected symbols exactly and reject absent, empty, duplicate, or ambiguous selections.
      ☑ Determine complete function boundaries for every supported object format; include blocks after early returns and avoid treating a return as an end-of-function delimiter.
      ☑ Preserve vector widths, register aliases, operands, immediates, and control-flow relationships.
      ☑ Preserve relocation/target information where required and expose referenced constant contents when a contract checks them.
      ☑ Remove only presentation details irrelevant to the selected checks; reject unrecognized formats instead of producing a successful empty extraction.
      ☑ Retain full disassembly and extracted bodies at case/configuration-specific paths suitable for diagnostics.
    ☑ Add extraction harness cases under tests/codegen/harness.
      ☑ Cover valid straight-line, early-return/multiple-block, relocation-bearing, and return-only functions.
      ☑ Reject missing/ambiguous function names and empty or malformed extraction inputs.
      ☑ Demonstrate that instructions after the first return, different vector widths, distinct operands, and relevant targets survive extraction.
      ☑ Cover required referenced constants and unsupported object/disassembly formats.
    ☑ Validate with synthetic harness inputs and representative real objects from each supported compiler driver/object format; record these as distinct evidence categories.
      ☑ Use provisional, explicitly recorded compilation settings for these probes; do not claim low-optimization qualification before Phase 4.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☑ Extraction harness positives and deliberate negatives behave as specified.
      ☑ Real-object evidence confirms supported formats and complete-body inspection without relying on the retired lossy normalizer.
      ☑ Required tools can be selected reproducibly in the intended validation environments.

    Evidence and Review:
      - Traceability and commands: docs/codegen/FunctionExtraction.md and docs/codegen/ToolProvisioning.md.
      - LLVM owns bounded disassembly and numeric constant-section dumps; custom instruction/body and byte parsers were removed.
      - MSVC 19.44.35228 and clang-cl 22.1.8: 27/27 standalone harness checks passed each, including assembled COFF/ELF and invalid-input regressions.
      - GCC 13.2.1, GCC 14.2.0, and Clang 22.1.3: six fresh object checks passed per container using LLVM 18.1.8, 20.1.8, and 22.1.3 respectively.
      - Exact Windows SDK/FileCheck provisioning recipe passed locally with LLVM 22.1.8; Linux provisioning, explicit selection, FileCheck positive/negative checks, and no-Python/lit checks passed.
      - Receipts: out/codegen-tools-provisioned, out/codegen-container-evidence, and out/codegen-extraction-probe/harness-*-wrapper-ctest.log.
      - Second review and independent audit-stage-completion found no unresolved required issues for this phase's scope.
      - Evidence is representative x64 extraction with provisional flags. Legacy adjacent x86 gates remain unchanged; full Windows-container/CI execution, production contracts and cutover remain later-phase work.

  Phase 3 - Implement Shared Contracts and CTest Registration (Historical Baseline):
    Depends on: Phase 1 inventory and Phase 2 extraction/tool validation.
    Outcome: a reusable build-and-check pipeline that enforces primary and supplemental requirements on shared fixture objects.

    Revision status:
      - Checked items below record completed work under the earlier proposal. They do not establish completion of the approved Catch2 replacement.
      - Phase 3A supersedes separate CMake check scheduling, pass-marker bookkeeping, and qualification tests while retaining LLVM extraction, shared rule files, and applicable evidence.

    ☑ Implement cmake/codegen/CheckInstructions.cmake for rule application and FileCheck invocation.
      ☑ Define reusable rule files under tests/codegen/contracts and method-specific expectations beside their fixtures.
      ☑ Add a small whole-body instruction allowlist helper only where FileCheck alone would make the rule unnecessarily obscure.
      ☑ Implement required/forbidden patterns, counts, bounded sequences, and captured operand relationships without replacing all registers with a common placeholder.
      ☑ Ensure forbidden patterns cover the complete function, and exact counts exclude extra occurrences outside the counted sequence.
      ☑ Run primary and supplemental expectations independently on the same body without cross-invocation captures or artificial ordering constraints.
      ☑ Scope compiler-specific allowances narrowly; model bounded /GS behavior and stronger no-stack/no-call facts as supplemental requirements where appropriate, never as overrides of shared rules.
    ☑ Implement cmake/development/CodegenTests.cmake as the shared fixture-target and CTest registration helper.
      ☑ Expand one primary definition across the applicable matrix; register only supplemental facts that apply to the selected compiler/configuration.
      ☑ Share the compiled object between primary and supplemental executions; do not create duplicate compilation targets for supplemental cases.
      ☑ Give CTest results stable primary/supplemental identities and configuration labels; require all applicable checks for a qualified case result.
      ☑ Parameterize type, width, ISA, extent, immediates, FMA, ABI, and configuration without compiler-specific copies of the whole case.
      ☑ Support ABI fixture groups while preserving exact identities and results for the required emitted functions.
    ☑ Integrate a limited pilot target with the existing build-before-test workflow without replacing legacy qualification gates yet.
      ☑ Compile through CMake/Ninja with normal source/header dependencies; CTest checks already-built objects rather than rebuilding each case.
      ☑ Reuse or adapt existing build freshness/provenance checks; fail clearly for missing or stale objects, tools, or configuration evidence.
      ☑ Keep parallel output paths distinct and shared object inputs read-only.
      ☑ Report case/function identity, compiler/tool identities, effective options, configuration, selected contracts, failed expectations, and artifact locations; retain FileCheck output and disassembly.
    ☑ Validate harness rule enforcement and primary/supplemental composition.
      ☑ Prove rejection of forbidden instructions after early returns, extra counted operations, wrong widths/immediates, broken captured operands, and incorrect required targets.
      ☑ Prove valid constant/identity and return-only cases can pass their contracts while empty extraction cannot.
      ☑ Prove a failed primary cannot be hidden by a passing supplement, and a failed applicable supplement cannot be hidden by a passing primary.
      ☑ Prove missing required primary/supplemental registrations or incorrect applicability cannot silently reduce expected coverage.
      ☑ Demonstrate reuse and isolation through focused parallel CTest execution, and verify incremental builds do not recompile unchanged fixtures unnecessarily.
    ☑ Prove the complete pipeline with representative register-only, memory, partial-invariant, and ABI production-code cases across the supported drivers, using explicit provisional settings.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☑ Harness and compiled pilot results prove extraction, rule enforcement, additive cases, failure reporting, and artifact isolation.
      ☑ Case declarations are concise and shared; primary checks remain mandatory on all declared supported compilers.
      ☑ CTest executes FileCheck without Python/lit or dependence on paired raw implementations.

    Evidence and Review:
      - Contract traceability, reproduction and evidence: docs/codegen/InstructionContracts.md.
      - Shared CTest/FileCheck primary and additive checks reuse eight compiled objects per driver; exact ABI member identities and constant-mask contents are retained.
      - MSVC 19.44.35228: 44/44; clang-cl 22.1.8: 46/46; GCC 14.2.0: 44/44; GNU-style Clang 22.1.3: 46/46 pilot checks passed.
      - Native top-level CUSTOM integration: 49/49 including five ownership checks; full native harness: 68/68 including 41 instruction/registration/composition checks.
      - Qualified-only selection included every mandatory check. Two unchanged builds per driver performed no fixture compilation or receipt regeneration.
      - Missing/stale inputs, missing or misapplied registrations, failed primaries/supplements, and deliberate instruction-rule mutations were rejected.
      - Second review and independent audit-stage-completion found no unresolved required issues. git diff --check passed.
      - Receipts: out/codegen-contract-evidence. Settings remain provisional; compatibility-version matrix, final optimization policy, full corpus and CI/cutover qualification remain later work.

  Phase 3A - Replace Outer Orchestration with Readable Catch2 Fixtures:
    Depends on: Phases 1-3 and the approved Catch2 design in the revised proposal.
    Outcome: the representative pilot runs through the existing Catch2 test system with readable definitions and no separate CMake result-marker protocol.

    Existing evidence, not completion:
      - docs/codegen/Catch2Prototype.md records the isolated transfer128 experiment: MSVC and GCC14 each passed four tests, including deliberate negatives; injected primary/supplement failures rejected independently.
      - The prototype retains existing extraction and freshness scripts. It does not establish full pilot parity, complete driver coverage, production process handling, or final CI integration.

    ☐ Reconcile codegen/MigrationInventory.md, IntegrationInventory.md, and InstructionContracts.md with the revised proposal, preserving case identities, required facts, and validation ownership.
    ☐ Implement shared support under tests/codegen/support with separate responsibilities for fixture lifetime, inspected-function assertions, and process execution.
      ☐ Define readable TEST_CASE_METHOD declarations beside each operation family; expose the inspected symbol, primary rule files, and named compiler selectors without exit-code or artifact bookkeeping.
      ☐ Implement nonfatal check/check_for semantics so every primary and applicable supplement reports and any failure fails the composite Catch2 test; inspection failure must fail clearly before matching.
      ☐ Select supplements by explicit compiler/driver/version/configuration applicability, permitting named shared compiler sets; prohibit positional compiler-rule mappings and primary-rule suppression.
      ☐ Parameterize geometry, ISA, extent, immediates, FMA, ABI, and configuration through fixture metadata, keeping each primary definition authored once.
      ☐ Keep FileCheck matching state independent per rule and reuse one complete input per function; preserve exact ABI member identities and constant-data checks.
    ☐ Retain the LLVM-based inspection wrapper and build-owned freshness receipts, removing only superseded outer orchestration.
      ☐ Verify selected tool identities, inputs, objects, and configuration before inspection or cached reuse; preserve missing/stale-input failures without per-rule marker files.
      ☐ Key process-local inspection reuse by object/function/configuration and account for separate CTest processes; isolate parallel artifact directories and define diagnostic retention/cleanup.
      ☐ Capture FileCheck diagnostics directly into Catch2 with case/function, primary/supplement identity, compiler/tools, effective options, configuration, and artifact locations.
      ☐ Provide shell-free Windows/Linux process handling with correct argument quoting, Unicode Windows paths, launch/read/exit errors, cleanup, and timeout behavior; test failures and paths with spaces/non-ASCII characters.
    ☐ Adapt CodegenTests.cmake and thin suite build helpers to build fixture objects and Catch2 runners, supply explicit metadata, and use existing Catch2 discovery and development ownership.
      ☐ Keep CTest test names stable by logical case/configuration, with primary/supplement identities visible in Catch2 diagnostics; no separately scheduled supplemental or qualified-result tests are required.
      ☐ Keep build-before-test behavior and ordinary incremental dependencies; supplements must not duplicate object compilation.
      ☐ Validate expected case/configuration/rule applicability independently of discovered tests; missing definitions or omitted/misapplied supplements must fail coverage validation.
    ☐ Port the full representative pilot and applicable harness negatives to the Catch2 runner, retaining provisional compiler settings and legacy qualification gates.
      ☐ Exercise register-only, memory, partial-invariant, constant/identity, return-only, and ABI fixture-group cases on MSVC, clang-cl, GCC, and GNU-style Clang; distinguish exercised versions from remaining compatibility coverage.
      ☐ Reproduce wrong widths/immediates, broken operands/targets, extra counted operations, forbidden work after returns, missing/ambiguous/empty extraction, and primary/supplement failure isolation.
      ☐ Prove actual composite-test failure for each required rule category, including passing-primary/failing-supplement and failing-primary/passing-supplement scenarios.
      ☐ Validate missing/stale inputs, tool errors/timeouts, expected coverage, filtered selection, parallel artifact isolation, process-local reuse, and two unchanged builds without fixture recompilation or receipt regeneration.
    ☐ Remove obsolete PrepareCase.cmake, RunCase.cmake, CheckInstructions.cmake, VerifyCaseResults.cmake and associated manifests/markers once all their pilot/harness consumers have validated replacements; reconcile references before removal.
      ☐ Retain any independently needed extraction, input-receipt, and allowlist responsibilities; do not remove the legacy wrapper/raw gates scheduled for Phase 7.
      ☐ Remove the isolated catch2-prototype project after its useful checks and evidence have been incorporated into the maintained runner.
    ☐ Review test definitions for readability against the proposal example and update reproduction/diagnostic documentation to describe the actual Catch2 interface.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ The complete representative pilot and required harness checks pass through Catch2 on the declared drivers with independent expected-coverage validation.
      ☐ Definitions state function, shared rules, and named supplemental applicability; matching remains LLVM/FileCheck-owned and all required failures propagate to one composite result.
      ☐ Process handling, freshness, diagnostics, parallel isolation, incremental behavior, and development integration have evidence; obsolete outer-runner plumbing and the isolated prototype are removed.
      ☐ A second proposal-to-implementation review confirms parity without reusing historical CMake-runner results as Catch2 qualification.

  Phase 4 - Establish Optimization and Configuration Contracts:
    Depends on: Phase 3A's validated Catch2 compiled pilot and trusted checks.
    Outcome: an evidence-backed configuration policy that can be used for the complete fixture migration.

    ☐ Evaluate low-optimization settings on the same representative fixture sources for every supported driver.
      ☐ Evaluate GCC/Clang -O1 as the initial candidate and select explicit MSVC optimization/inlining options; do not equate MSVC /O1 with GCC/Clang -O1 by name.
      ☐ Record the final effective compiler options and ensure inherited build flags do not silently select a different optimization level.
      ☐ Separate optimization from assertions, sanitizers, ISA, FMA, stack protection, and calling-convention settings.
      ☐ Preserve production attributes; do not add fixture-only force-inlining or barriers to manufacture passing output.
    ☐ Define and verify the production-optimization companion configuration using the same fixtures and applicable contracts.
      ☐ Run its checks independently; do not infer production results from low-optimization passes.
    ☐ Decide and document meaningful wholly unoptimized coverage and diagnostic-only observations.
      ☐ Account explicitly for disabled inlining, including MSVC /Od and /Ob0, and legitimate compiler-generated storage.
      ☐ Do not impose a universal no-call/no-stack contract on configurations where the selected settings conflict with it.
      ☐ Do not describe unoptimized output as a worst-case performance bound.
    ☐ Review each failed proposed contract and record whether it exposes a fixture issue, implementation issue, compiler/configuration limitation, or unsupported claim.
      ☐ Keep production changes outside this plan and record unresolved failures; do not silently relax primary requirements or lose established stronger supplemental facts.
    ☐ Add the decided configuration dimensions to the shared registrations and migration inventory, keeping one source and primary definition per method.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ Exact low-optimization settings are supported by real compiled pilot evidence for each required driver.
      ☐ Production companion checks have their own results, and the selected unoptimized boundary is explicit.
      ☐ No required configuration decision or applicable pilot-contract failure remains unresolved; documented diagnostic limitations are distinguished from failed required checks.

  Phase 5 - Migrate Register and RegisterMask Cases:
    Depends on: Phases 1-4, including Phase 3A.
    Outcome: complete mapped Register/RegisterMask instruction contracts validated alongside the legacy gates.

    ☐ Rewrite Register fixtures and readable Catch2 case definitions under tests/codegen/register by method or operation family, with thin build configuration in cmake/development/RegisterCodegen.cmake.
      ☐ Migrate every available isolated operation/type/width case from the common and modulus type matrices.
      ☐ Migrate construction, observation, transfers, special members, mutation, reassignment, and composed expressions.
      ☐ Migrate specialized arithmetic/reductions, FMA enabled/disabled cases, rearrangements, conversions, bit casts, and width changes.
      ☐ Migrate immediate and complete shifts, including zero/count-boundary cases, preserving existing targeted instruction properties.
      ☐ Migrate register-pressure and opaque-call contexts without artificial fixture spills or barriers.
      ☐ Migrate explicit and consumer ABI boundaries, using caller/callee fixture groups where needed; preserve platform-default observations with their declared qualification limits.
    ☐ For every migrated method, define the primary facts once and attach only independently applicable compiler-specific supplements using the same fixture output.
      ☐ Retain genuine stronger facts through narrow supplements rather than lowering every compiler to the least restrictive expectations.
      ☐ Avoid implementing the operation through a raw Api or intrinsic mirror; public observation calls remain allowed where needed to expose results.
    ☐ Update the inventory and expected-case registrations as each family is migrated; keep unsupported availability cells distinct from missing tests.
    ☐ Build and run the migrated instruction cases across their complete applicable compiler/ISA/type/configuration matrix, retaining per-case evidence.
    ☐ Run proportionate existing behavioral and ABI/consumer checks for the migrated families; report these separately from instruction-contract results.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ Every Register/RegisterMask inventory entry has a validated replacement or an unresolved entry that prevents phase completion.
      ☐ Shared definitions cover the full declared matrix without duplicated compiler suites; constant/identity and ABI fixture-group cases follow the approved clarifications.
      ☐ No legacy gate or raw fixture needed by remaining migrations has been removed.

  Phase 6 - Migrate Partial Registers and Remaining Instruction Cases:
    Depends on: Phase 5's completed Register migration and the shared infrastructure from Phases 1-4, including Phase 3A.
    Outcome: all remaining fixture and instruction-contract cases have validated replacements through the pilot pipeline; aggregate and pipeline-consumer migration remains in Phase 7.

    ☐ Rewrite PartialRegister and PartialRegisterMask fixtures and readable Catch2 definitions under tests/codegen/partial-register, keeping cmake/development/PartialRegisterCodegen.cmake as thin build configuration.
      ☐ Migrate predicate/invariant boundaries, value operations, arithmetic/specialized results, general operations, and ABI cases from the inventory.
      ☐ Preserve native-import sanitation, inactive-suffix projection, inactive-divisor neutralization, logical extents, and checked public observation boundaries where required.
      ☐ Cover selected active extents and cross-width/type results explicitly; do not treat inactive-lane invariant work as unwanted wrapper overhead.
      ☐ Reuse primary fixtures for narrow supplements; do not recreate retained hash categories as duplicate compiler-specific full cases.
    ☐ Migrate API partial-transfer and method-attribute checks that use the retiring machinery, retaining their separate purposes and validation ownership.
      ☐ Inspect all inventoried references to CompareRegisterCodegen.cmake, retained-profile data, and associated validators; validate replacements for every in-scope fixture and instruction-contract case through the pilot pipeline.
      ☐ Identify aggregate, manifest, index, and validation policy/isolation consumers separately in the inventory and assign their migration and validation to Phase 7.
      ☐ Preserve platform-default ABI observations and diagnostic configurations without promoting them to stronger performance claims.
    ☐ Build and run all newly migrated cases across their complete applicable compiler/ISA/extent/configuration matrix.
    ☐ Run appropriate existing active-/inactive-lane behavioral, precondition, and ABI/consumer checks; retain their independent oracles and distinguish their results from codegen evidence.
    ☐ Reconcile the migration inventory's fixture and instruction-contract entries against expected registrations and produced case results, including all applicable supplements; retain pipeline-consumer entries as outstanding Phase 7 work.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ Every in-scope fixture and instruction-contract case has a validated replacement on its required matrix through the pilot pipeline, with no unexplained missing primary or supplemental result.
      ☐ Required partial-register semantics and distinct method-attribute/API-transfer evidence remain covered.
      ☐ The inventory supplies the case evidence required to begin Phase 7; legacy machinery cannot be removed until Phase 7's pipeline-consumer validation gate also passes.

  Phase 7 - Cut Over the Pipeline and Remove Obsolete Machinery:
    Depends on: completed Phase 5 and Phase 6 matrix evidence for every inventoried fixture and instruction-contract case; pipeline-consumer migration and validation are owned by this phase.
    Outcome: the normal build/test workflow uses the replacement contracts exclusively and preserves useful diagnostics and provenance.

    ☐ Change the ordinary build/test aggregates and scripts to select the validated replacement targets and CTest cases.
      ☐ Update tools/Build.ps1, tools/Run-Tests.ps1, tools/Record-Codegen.ps1, validation manifests/indexes, or their owning helpers only where the inventory identifies a dependency on the old format or target names.
      ☐ Preserve build-before-test freshness, incremental compilation, diagnostic selection, clear missing-tool/input failures, and distinct required versus observational results.
      ☐ Update validation inventory/policy/isolation checks to recognize the new owners and coverage without weakening non-codegen isolation or unrelated checks.
    ☐ Finalize local/container/Windows CI provisioning and artifact publication for the selected tools and new case outputs.
      ☐ Keep the existing compiler/driver coverage, including the distinct supported clang-cl versions; do not silently substitute toolchains or omit unavailable matrix cells.
      ☐ Publish full disassembly, extracted bodies, FileCheck failures, and compiler/configuration provenance from the new locations.
    ☐ Pass the pipeline-consumer validation gate before removing legacy machinery.
      ☐ Validate migrated aggregates, build/test/diagnostic scripts, manifests, indexes, and validation inventory/policy/isolation checks through the replacement workflow.
      ☐ Verify required case coverage, freshness failures, diagnostic selection, and artifact publication; retain evidence for every inventoried pipeline consumer.
      ☐ Keep legacy machinery available until all required replacement consumers pass; unresolved consumer failures block removal.
    ☐ Remove retired paired raw fixtures, duplicate wrapper/raw scaffolding, and full-profile equality gates only after the pipeline-consumer validation gate passes and all references have been reconciled with the completed inventory.
      ☐ Remove PartialRegisterCodegenProfiles.json and its retained-profile loader, hash-acceptance branches, obsolete comparison indexes, and validators only when no remaining consumer needs them.
      ☐ Retire CompareRegisterCodegen.cmake and replaced specialized comparison scripts once every consumer has moved.
      ☐ Preserve independently useful input/artifact hashes for freshness and independent scalar correctness references.
      ☐ Remove temporary coexistence switches and pilot-only plumbing; retain useful migrated pilot cases as ordinary tests.
    ☐ Update qualification documents, the implementation matrix, README, and reproduction instructions with the cutover so executable policy and claims describe the same boundary.
      ☐ Describe shared primary cases, additive supplements, configuration roles, permitted overhead, and diagnostic exclusions.
      ☐ Remove obsolete wrapper/raw equality claims and retained-hash acceptance instructions without introducing claims of semantic proof, optimal code, or measured speed.
    ☐ Verify a focused build/test/diagnostic workflow on the new owners and audit references for orphaned targets, obsolete policy data, missing artifacts, and unintended runtime dependencies.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ Normal execution uses Catch2 composite assertions scheduled by CTest and matched by FileCheck; no retained wrapper/raw hash remains an acceptance mechanism.
      ☐ All legacy removals have both case evidence from Phases 5-6 and pipeline-consumer evidence from the pre-removal gate, with no dangling consumers; temporary migration paths are removed.
      ☐ Pipeline ownership, diagnostic behavior, documentation, and artifact publication agree before final qualification.

  Phase 8 - Qualify the Final Tree and Reconcile Evidence:
    Depends on: Phase 7 cutover and cleanup.
    Outcome: final qualification evidence reflects the actual replacement-only tree and its documented claims.

    ☐ Run the complete applicable native/container/CI compiler matrix against the final tree using the project entry points selected in Phase 7.
      ☐ Run required low-optimization and production-optimization instruction checks independently, plus the selected wholly unoptimized contracts and diagnostic observations.
      ☐ Require the exact expected primary cases and applicable supplements across all supported dimensions; distinguish unexecuted cells from unsupported cells and passing cells.
      ☐ Record real local/container runs separately from Windows CI results; do not infer one environment's success from another's artifacts.
    ☐ Run the existing required correctness, constexpr, availability, compile-failure, layout, header/consumer, and package checks from the qualification matrix.
      ☐ Preserve existing sanitizer coverage where required; do not introduce a blanket sanitizer codegen-performance requirement.
      ☐ Confirm installed consumers gain no FileCheck, disassembler, Python, or lit runtime dependency.
    ☐ Re-run the harness positives/negatives and case-coverage checks in their required supported environments; preserve failures as failures rather than refreshing acceptance data.
    ☐ Reconcile final commands, tool identities, results, logs, and artifacts with the migration inventory, qualification documents, implementation matrix, README, and CI definitions.
    ☐ Perform a second source and documentation review for duplicated full compiler tests, weakened primaries, missing supplements, lossy extraction, unsupported claims, and obsolete compatibility scaffolding.

    Completion Criteria:
      ☐ Every required final-tree qualification result exists and passes; evidence from a pre-cleanup tree or an unavailable environment does not satisfy this gate.
      ☐ Behavioral, ABI/consumer/package, harness, and instruction-contract evidence are distinguished and agree with their documented scope.
      ☐ All acceptance criteria below are satisfied without silently deferred coverage or unresolved required failures.

  Proposal Traceability:
    - Decision summary and evidence boundaries: execution contract, scope/non-goals, and Phases 1, 7, 8.
    - Single production fixture, constant/identity lowering, and ABI fixture groups: Phases 1, 3, 5, 6.
    - Primary/supplemental ownership, reusable rules, counts, operands, and matching scope: Phases 1, 3, 5, 6.
    - Complete function extraction, relevant relocations/constants, and failure on missing/unsupported input: Phase 2, checked again in Phases 3 and 8.
    - Optimization choices and limited claims: Phase 4, with corpus execution in Phases 5, 6, 8.
    - Readable Catch2 definitions, named selectors, composite nonfatal assertions, process handling, and removal of outer CMake bookkeeping: Phase 3A, expanded in Phases 5-6 and qualified in Phase 8.
    - CMake/Catch2/CTest ownership, freshness, parallel artifacts, provisioning, and no Python/lit: Phases 2, 3A, 7, 8; Phase 3 is historical baseline evidence.
    - Coverage inventory, migration, removal, diagnostics, and qualification documents: Phases 1, 5, 6, 7, 8.

  Final Acceptance:
    ☐ Every source-proposal requirement is mapped to completed work and appropriate evidence; any unresolved required item remains unchecked.
    ☐ Each primary method case is authored once across its supported matrix; supplements contain only additional compiler-specific facts and cannot replace or weaken it.
    ☐ Each tested operation or composition has one production-code implementation under test, allowing legitimate constant/identity results and necessary ABI caller/callee groups without duplicated algorithms.
    ☐ Complete-body inspection and rule enforcement reject the specified adversarial cases and missing coverage, including failures in either primary or supplemental checks.
    ☐ Low-optimization settings, production companion checks, and the selected unoptimized boundary have explicit evidence and accurate claims.
    ☐ Catch2 owns readable composite instruction tests, CTest schedules them, and FileCheck matches rules, with no new Python/lit requirement or library/runtime tooling dependency.
    ☐ Named selectors cannot suppress primary rules or required supplements; no separate CMake pass-marker/qualification protocol or isolated prototype remains.
    ☐ Retired raw duplicates, retained-hash acceptance, obsolete gates, and temporary coexistence plumbing are removed without deleting independent behavioral references.
    ☐ Required final-tree codegen, behavioral, ABI/consumer/package, and CI evidence is complete, and documentation matches executed policy.
